{"batchcomplete": true, "query": {"pages": [{"pageid": 1181168, "ns": 0, "title": "Sacsayhuamán", "extract": "Sacsayhuamán ( SACK-sy-wuh-mən; Spanish pronunciation: [saksajwaˈman]) or Saksaywaman (from Quechua Saksay waman pukara, pronounced [ˈsaksaj ˈwaman], lit. 'fortress of the royal falcon or hawk') is a citadel on the northern outskirts of the city of Cusco, Peru, the historic capital of the Inca Empire. The site is an important example of Inca architecture and sits at an altitude of 3,701 metres (12,142 ft).\nThe Spanish chroniclers recorded that within the Inca's oral history, the fortress was said to be built during the reign of Sapa Inca Pachacuti and his successors, Topa Inca Yupanqui and Huayna Capac. Dry stone walls constructed of huge stones were built on the site, with the workers carefully cutting the boulders to fit them together tightly without mortar.\nIn 1983, Cusco and Sacsayhuamán together were designated as sites on the UNESCO World Heritage List, for international recognition and protection. The archeological site is now a tourist destination.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:42:45Z", "lastrevid": 1365739731, "length": 25224, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q828336"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.50777778, "lon": -71.98222222, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=TWLjONT3uYQC"}, {"url": "https://www.lavanguardia.com/ocio/viajes/20170621/423527468224/21-junio-solsticios-verano-invierno-stonehenge-midsummer-cuzco-inti-raymi-celebraciones.html"}, {"url": "https://www.worldbyisa.com/20-things-to-do-in-cusco/"}, {"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/273/"}, {"url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/Sacsayhuaman/"}, {"url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48785/48785-h/48785-h.htm#CHAPTER_LI"}, {"url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna23626672"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/peruincidentsoft00squi/page/430/mode/2up"}, {"url": "https://www.ticketmachupicchu.com/fortress-sacsayhuaman/"}, {"url": "https://www.illa-a.org/cd/diccionarios/VocabvlarioQqichuaDeHolguin.pdf"}, {"url": "https://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7642"}, {"url": "https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/americas/7295754.stm"}, {"url": "https://worlds-greatest-riddles.com/sacsayhuaman.php"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n&params=13_30_28_S_71_58_56_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n&params=13_30_28_S_71_58_56_W_type:landmark"}, {"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/273"}, {"url": "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv6jmwp4.8"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.2307%2Fj.ctv6jmwp4.8"}, {"url": "https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/americas/8480013.stm"}, {"url": "https://www.earthasweknowit.com/pages/inca_construction"}, {"url": "https://www.wdl.org/en/item/10096/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260204132800/https://www.earthasweknowit.com/pages/inca_construction"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210624202147/https://www.worldhistory.org/Sacsayhuaman/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20211024040024/https://www.wdl.org/en/item/10096/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20051001012521/https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/273/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20200614103559/http://www.worldbyisa.com/20-things-to-do-in-cusco/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250825120754/https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48785/48785-h/48785-h.htm#CHAPTER_LI"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250425012131/https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna23626672"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240923053721/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/americas/8480013.stm"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230802124420/https://www.lavanguardia.com/ocio/viajes/20170621/423527468224/21-junio-solsticios-verano-invierno-stonehenge-midsummer-cuzco-inti-raymi-celebraciones.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20161002000802/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7642"}, {"url": "https://repositorio.cultura.gob.pe/bitstream/handle/CULTURA/1800/Ley%20N%C2%B0%2023765.pdf"}]}, {"pageid": 1253138, "ns": 0, "title": "Religion in the Inca Empire", "extract": "The Inca religion was a group of beliefs and rites that were related to a mythological system evolving from pre-Inca times to Inca Empire. Faith in the Tawantinsuyu was manifested in every aspect of his life, work, festivities, ceremonies, etc. They were polytheists and there were local, regional and pan-regional divinities.\nIt has been noted that aspects of the Andean religion extend well beyond the border of the former Inca Empire into the lands of the Mapuches and Huilliches in southern Chile, which has raised the hypothesis whether there is a prior dissemination of Andean religion from Tiwanaku.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T03:53:02Z", "lastrevid": 1367231092, "length": 37486, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religion_in_the_Inca_Empire", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Religion_in_the_Inca_Empire&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Religion_in_the_Inca_Empire", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q3238302"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.lib.berkeley.edu/ANTH/emeritus/rowe/pub/rowe.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20070305220022/http://philtar.ucsm.ac.uk/encyclopedia/latam/inca.html"}, {"url": "http://0-search.eb.com.tiger.coloradocollege.edu:80/eb/article-69447"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=sC1KDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA60"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=Kt4eBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA28"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=rOo9DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA75"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/handbookofincamy0000stee"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/incasthepeopleso00tere"}, {"url": "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305440310003195"}, {"url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/Inca_Religion/"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:162909245"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.jas.2010.09.009"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.3158%2F0071-4739(2007)188%5B1:THOSAL%5D2.0.CO;2"}, {"url": "https://ijels.com/detail/symbolic-communication-and-the-notion-of-pachamama-in-the-quechua-and-aimara-cultures/"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.22161%2Fijels.4.2.47"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0305-4403"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0190-9940"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/156911932"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/708564500"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/62805010"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/46449340"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0718-04622015000200012"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0718-22012013000200018"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0718-68942018000300121"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1093%2Foso%2F9780197542552.003.0002"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1057%2Fs41305-018-0100-4"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2011JArSc..38..323A"}]}, {"pageid": 1589830, "ns": 0, "title": "Sacred Valley", "extract": "The Sacred Valley of the Incas (Spanish: Valle Sagrado de los Incas; Quechua: Willka Qhichwa), or the Urubamba Valley, is a valley in the Andes of Peru, north of the Inca capital of Cusco. It is located in the present-day Peruvian region of Cusco. In colonial documents it was referred to as the \"Valley of Yucay\". The Sacred Valley was incorporated slowly into the Inca Empire during the period from 1000 to 1400.\nThe Sacred Valley is a major tourist destination. In 2019, 1.6 million people, the majority non-Peruvians, visited Machu Picchu, its most famous archaeological site. Many of the same tourists also visited other archaeological sites and modern towns in the Sacred Valley.\nStretching from Pisac to Ollantaytambo, this fertile valley is irrigated by the Urubamba River. The Chanapata civilisation first utilised this area starting at around 800 BCE because of its rich soil used for agriculture. The Qotacalla civilization lived in the Sacred Valley from 500 to 900 CE. The Killke civilization then flourished in the Sacred Valley from 900 until it was absorbed into the Incan Empire in 1420. The Incan Empire ruled this area for little more than 100 years until the arrival of the Spanish.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:42:50Z", "lastrevid": 1362238241, "length": 13389, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacred_Valley", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sacred_Valley&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacred_Valley", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2237464"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.33333333, "lon": -72.08333333, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.wikiservice.at/upload/UweChristianPlachetka/Tikpa.pdf"}, {"url": "http://macareo.pucp.edu.pe/~jearls/documentosPDF/theCharacter.PDF"}, {"url": "https://en.climate-data.org/location/44993/"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1525%2Faa.2002.104.3.846"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016%2FS0278-4165(03)00030-8"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1080%2F00043079.2007.10786358"}, {"url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/25067338"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:194099969"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Sacred_Valley&params=13_20_S_72_05_W_"}, {"url": "https://www.touristsecrets.com/destinations/central-south-america/peru/how-many-tourists-visit-machu-picchu-every-year/"}, {"url": "https://www.omniglot.com/writing/quechua.htm"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Sacred_Valley&params=13_20_S_72_05_W_type:river"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0004-3079"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20170517014832/http://macareo.pucp.edu.pe/~jearls/documentosPDF/theCharacter.PDF"}, {"url": "https://repositorioarchivos.cultura.gob.pe/bim_files/anexos/bim166815009.pdf"}]}, {"pageid": 1990573, "ns": 0, "title": "Samaipata, Bolivia", "extract": "Samaipata or Samaypata (Quechua samay to rest, pata elevated place / above, at the top / edge, bank (of a river), shore) is a small town in the Florida Province of the Santa Cruz Department in Bolivia. It has a subtropical climate and an altitude of 1600–1800 m. It lies about 120 kilometers to the southwest of the city of Santa Cruz de la Sierra in the foothills of the Andes on the way to Sucre. It is a popular resort for the inhabitants of Santa Cruz due to its cool climate. As such, there is a regular bus and taxi service to Santa Cruz. The town is small, with numerous vibrant colonial buildings and narrow cobbled streets. It is located close to several tourist attractions such as El Fuerte de Samaipata, the Amboro National Park, El Codo de los Andes, Cuevas waterfalls, vineyards, rapids and lagoons, as well as well-preserved colonial towns such as Vallegrande, Pampagrande, Postrervalle, Santiago del Valle, Pucará, and others. It is also the first stop in the several tourist trails to Sucre, Potosi, and the Che Guevara route.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:42:55Z", "lastrevid": 1324219038, "length": 17296, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samaipata,_Bolivia", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Samaipata,_Bolivia&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samaipata,_Bolivia", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q1014461"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -18.17944444, "lon": -63.87555556, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.samaipata.info/"}, {"url": "http://www.eldeber.com.bo/anteriores/20030601/escenas_5.html"}, {"url": "http://www.andes-bolivia.ch/ingles/samay.html"}, {"url": "http://www.eldeber.com.bo/2007/2007-06-24/vernotaescenas.php?id=996"}, {"url": "http://sites.google.com/site/climaenbolivia/clima-en-bolivia/clima-en-santa-cruz/clima-en-samaipata"}, {"url": "http://www.la-razon.com/suplementos/escape/Samaipata-vendimia-crucena_0_1796820385.html"}, {"url": "http://www.censosbolivia.bo/comunitaria/comunitaria/mpComunitariaVer.aspx?Depto=07&Prov=09&Seccion=01"}, {"url": "http://www.vicepresidencia.gob.bo/Se-inicia-la-celebracion-del-188"}, {"url": "http://www.winesofbolivia.com/region-overview/?santa-cruz"}, {"url": "http://www.uvairenda.com/"}, {"url": "http://www.weatherbase.com/weather/weather.php3?s=600113&cityname=samaipata-Santa=Cruz-Bolivia&units=metric,"}, {"url": "http://www.weatherbase.com/weather/weatherall.php3?s%3D600113%26units%3D"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20150910194005/http://www.weatherbase.com/weather/weatherall.php3?s=600113&units="}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20081206170138/http://www.eldeber.com.bo/anteriores/20030601/escenas_5.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20130628192647/http://www.andes-bolivia.ch/ingles/samay.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20110716055240/http://www.eldeber.com.bo/2007/2007-06-24/vernotaescenas.php?id=996"}, {"url": "http://www.bodegaslandsua.com/"}, {"url": "https://d-nb.info/gnd/4392452-9"}, {"url": "https://viaf.org/viaf/123175659"}, {"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/883"}, {"url": "https://www.wine-and-travel.com/destinations/bolivia"}, {"url": "https://www.idref.fr/113758952"}, {"url": "https://id.loc.gov/authorities/n82058387"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Samaipata,_Bolivia&params=18_10_46_S_63_52_32_W_region:BO-S_type:city(3500)"}, {"url": "https://www.nli.org.il/en/authorities/987007566818805171"}, {"url": "https://lux.collections.yale.edu/view/place/2401dda2-cebf-477e-9f79-82f9cb1cae04"}]}, {"pageid": 5946317, "ns": 0, "title": "San Agustín Archaeological Park", "extract": "The San Agustín Archaeological Park (Spanish: Parque Arqueológico de San Agustín) is a large archaeological area located near the town of San Agustín in Huila Department in Colombia. The park contains the largest collection of religious monuments and megalithic sculptures in Latin America and is considered the world's largest necropolis. Belonging to the San Agustin culture, it was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1995.\nThe dates of the statues are uncertain, but they are believed to have been carved between 5–400 AD. The origin of the carvers remains a mystery, as the site is largely unexcavated.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:43:37Z", "lastrevid": 1365739903, "length": 8124, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Agust%C3%ADn_Archaeological_Park", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=San_Agust%C3%ADn_Archaeological_Park&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Agust%C3%ADn_Archaeological_Park", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2887224"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": 1.91666667, "lon": -76.23333333, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://academiahistoria.org.co/boletin/index.php/boletin857/article/download/41/45"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/ecuadorgalapagos00rath/page/268"}, {"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/744"}, {"url": "https://hyperallergic.com/800333/colombia-demands-the-return-of-its-san-agustin-statues/"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=San_Agust%C3%ADn_Archaeological_Park&params=1_55_N_76_14_W_"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.22380%2F2539472X43"}, {"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ab5Bz2vk7ek"}, {"url": "https://www.elespectador.com/noticias/cultura/patrimonio-de-humanidad-colombia-articulo-446322"}, {"url": "https://www.eltiempo.com/archivo/documento/CMS-3930563"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210225195818/http://academiahistoria.org.co/boletin/index.php/boletin857/article/download/41/45"}]}, {"pageid": 7337835, "ns": 0, "title": "Revash", "extract": "The Revash funerary complex is located in Peru's Santo Tomás District, part of Luya Province, which is approximately 60 km to the south of Chachapoyas. The funeral buildings are located at an altitude of 2,800 m above sea level. They are within the calcareous rock formation of Cerro Carbón, located in the margin left side of the vale of Alto Utcubamba. The San José de Laumar River serves as the Southeastern border.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:43:50Z", "lastrevid": 1364718514, "length": 7079, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revash", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Revash&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revash", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2088559"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -6.5407, "lon": -77.8562, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.wmf.org/project/revash-funerary-complex"}, {"url": "https://www.jstor.org/action/doBasicSearch?Query=%22Revash%22&acc=on&wc=on"}, {"url": "https://www.traveller.com.au/the-mausoleums-of-revash-perus-most-intriguing-attractions-most-visitors-miss-h1mi4y"}, {"url": "https://archive.archaeology.org/0801/abstracts/chachapoya.html"}, {"url": "https://www.nationalgeographic.co.uk/travel/2019/08/walking-perus-cloud-warriors"}, {"url": "https://anthropology.ua.edu/blogs/gregbatchelder/category/ecotourism/"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=b2YmtAEACAAJ"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Revash&params=6.5407_S_77.8562_W_source:wikidata"}, {"url": "https://www.google.com/search?as_eq=wikipedia&q=%22Revash%22"}, {"url": "https://www.google.com/search?tbm=nws&q=%22Revash%22+-wikipedia&tbs=ar:1"}, {"url": "https://www.google.com/search?&q=%22Revash%22&tbs=bkt:s&tbm=bks"}, {"url": "https://www.google.com/search?tbs=bks:1&q=%22Revash%22+-wikipedia"}, {"url": "https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=%22Revash%22"}]}, {"pageid": 11800147, "ns": 0, "title": "Raqch'i", "extract": "Raqch'i (Quechua) is an Inca archaeological site in Peru located in the Cusco Region, Canchis Province, San Pedro District, near the populated place Raqch'i. It is 3480 meters above sea level and 110 kilometers from the city of Cuzco. It also known as the Temple of Wiracocha, one of its constituents. Both lie along the Vilcanota River. The site has experienced a recent increase in tourism in recent years, with 83,334 visitors to the site in 2006, up from 8,183 in 2000 and 452 in 1996.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:44:12Z", "lastrevid": 1364718464, "length": 14784, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Raqch%27i&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2876258"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -14.175, "lon": -71.36666667, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "https://doi.org/10.2307%2F482265"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Raqch'i&params=14_10_30_S_71_22_00_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Raqch'i&params=14_10_30_S_71_22_00_W_type:landmark"}, {"url": "https://www.congreso.gob.pe/museo/estadisticas.pdf"}, {"url": "https://www.inc-cusco.gob.pe/PlanesMaestros/Planes/PM_Raqchi/Arqueologia01.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20080427004354/https://www.inc-cusco.gob.pe/PlanesMaestros/Planes/PM_Raqchi/Arqueologia01.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20081214051721/https://www.inc-cusco.gob.pe/planesmaestros/Planes/PM_Raqchi/"}]}, {"pageid": 39001237, "ns": 0, "title": "Rumicolca", "extract": "Rumicolca (possibly from Quechua rumi stone, qullqa, qulqa deposit, storehouse) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Quispicanchi Province, Lucre District. Rumicolca is situated near the archaeological sites Chuqi Pukyu and Pikillaqta, east of Lake Huacarpay.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:02Z", "lastrevid": 1364718703, "length": 5565, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Rumicolca&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2503884"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.61805556, "lon": -71.70722222, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://archaeology.about.com/od/wterms/qt/wari_empire.htm"}, {"url": "https://www.gigapan.com/gigapans/226566"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Rumicolca&params=13_37_05_S_71_42_26_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Rumicolca&params=13_37_05_S_71_42_26_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 41739805, "ns": 0, "title": "Rumiwasi", "extract": "Rumiwasi (Quechua rumi stone, wasi house, \"stone house\", hispanicized spelling Rumihuasi) or Phaqchayuq (Quechua phaqcha waterfall, -yuq a suffix to indicate ownership, \"the one with a waterfall\", hispanicized Phaqchayoc) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Cusco Province, San Sebastián District, north of the central square of San Sebastián.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:41Z", "lastrevid": 1364718716, "length": 1993, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumiwasi", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Rumiwasi&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumiwasi", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15949995"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.51944444, "lon": -71.94027778, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4762"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140204043145/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4762"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Rumiwasi&params=13_31_10_S_71_56_25_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Rumiwasi&params=13_31_10_S_71_56_25_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 41798976, "ns": 0, "title": "Qurimarka, Apurímac", "extract": "Qurimarka (Quechua quri gold, marka village / storey) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is situated in the Apurímac Region, Abancay Province, Huanipaca District.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:42Z", "lastrevid": 1364976198, "length": 2276, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qurimarka,_Apur%C3%ADmac", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Qurimarka,_Apur%C3%ADmac&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qurimarka,_Apur%C3%ADmac", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15950024"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=576"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20161024090257/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=576"}]}, {"pageid": 41816699, "ns": 0, "title": "Qurimarka, Cusco", "extract": "Qurimarka (Quechua quri gold, marka village / storey) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Urubamba Province, Ollantaytambo District. It is situated at the river Rayanniyuq (Rayanniyoc) and it belongs to the community Rayanniyuq.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:42Z", "lastrevid": 1364976200, "length": 2257, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qurimarka,_Cusco", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Qurimarka,_Cusco&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qurimarka,_Cusco", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15950041"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7192"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140220071902/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7192"}]}, {"pageid": 42377467, "ns": 0, "title": "Quriwayrachina, Ayacucho", "extract": "Quriwayrachina, Quri Wayrachina (Quechua quri gold, wayrachina a special oven for smelting metal, \"oven for smelting gold\"), Hatun Quriwayrachina or Hatun Quri Wayrachina (Quechua hatun big, also spelled Qatun Quriwayrachina) is an archaeological site of the Inca period in Peru located in the Ayacucho Region, Lucanas Province, Carmen Salcedo District. It lies near the mountain Inka Pallanka which is venerated as an apu by the people of the area. There are two platforms which are known as Hatun Quri Wayrachina and Huch'uy Quri Wayrachina (Quechua huch'uy small, also spelled Uchuy Quriwayrachina) by the locals.  On April 20, 2011, the site was declared a National Cultural Heritage by Resolución Viceministerial No. 459-2011-VMPCIC-MC.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:34Z", "lastrevid": 1364718359, "length": 2563, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Ayacucho", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Quriwayrachina,_Ayacucho&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Ayacucho", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q17086614"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://indigenasdelperu.files.wordpress.com/2015/09/diccionario-qeswa-academia-mayor-cuzco.pdf"}]}, {"pageid": 42377855, "ns": 0, "title": "Quriwayrachina, Anta", "extract": "Quriwayrachina or Quri Wayrachina (Quechua quri gold, wayrachina a special oven for smelting metal, \"oven for smelting gold\") is an archaeological site with agricultural terraces in Peru. It is situated in the Cusco Region, Anta Province. The terraces lie about 4 kilometres (2.5 mi) north of the terraces of Zurite.\nHiram Bingham III visited the site in April 1915, and by Paul Fejos in 1940", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:34Z", "lastrevid": 1364718355, "length": 2685, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Anta", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Quriwayrachina,_Anta&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Anta", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q17086613"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.drc-cusco.gob.pe/index.php/zonas-sitios-arqueologicos-anta/importancia-za-anta?showall=&start=7"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/lostcityofincass00bing/page/254"}, {"url": "https://indigenasdelperu.files.wordpress.com/2015/09/diccionario-qeswa-academia-mayor-cuzco.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140107111304/http://www.drc-cusco.gob.pe/index.php/zonas-sitios-arqueologicos-anta/importancia-za-anta?showall=&start=7"}]}, {"pageid": 47592129, "ns": 0, "title": "Runayoc", "extract": "Runayoc (possibly from Quechua runa person, -yuq a suffix) is an archaeological site in Peru on a mountain of that name. It is situated in the Huancavelica Region, Huaytará Province, Huaytará District. The ruins of Runayuq are situated at a height of about 3,400 m (11,200 ft).", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:58:10Z", "lastrevid": 1364978237, "length": 1777, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runayoc", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Runayoc&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runayoc", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q21044313"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4406"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20160807173256/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4406"}, {"url": "https://futatraw.ourproject.org/descargas/DicQuechuaBolivia.pdf"}]}, {"pageid": 54324302, "ns": 0, "title": "Rumicucho", "extract": "Rumicucho or Pucara de Rumicucho is an archaeological site of the Inca Empire in the parroquia of San Antonio de Pichincha, in Quito Canton, Pichincha Province. Ecuador.  Rumicucho is a pucara (hilltop fortress) located 23 kilometres (14 mi) in a straight-line distance north of the city of Quito at an elevation of 2,401 metres (7,877 ft). Rumicucho in the Quechua language means \"stone corner\", perhaps referring to its strategic location between the territory of the Yumbo people to the east and the chiefdoms of the Pais Caranqui to the north.\nThe Incas probably built Rumicucho between 1480 and 1500 and ruled this area until the Spanish conquest in the 1530s.\nRumicucho has also been called Lulumbamba (fertile plain), a reference to the valley to its west, now mostly urbanized but formerly intensely cultivated.  Rumicucho is a popular site for tourists to visit.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:59:56Z", "lastrevid": 1364718711, "length": 5108, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicucho", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Rumicucho&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicucho", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q6090782"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://www.ecostravel.com/en/ecuador/cities-destination/rumicucho.php"}, {"url": "https://www.archaeological.org/fieldwork/afob/4187"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Rumicucho&params=0_00_43_N_78_25_53_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 74760889, "ns": 0, "title": "Real Alto", "extract": "Real Alto is an archaeological site in Chanduy valley of Ecuador, located between the cities Guayaquil and Salinas. It was settled between 6000 and 3800 BC.\nThe site was first identified by archeologist Jorge Marcos in 1971. Since then, it was investigated by a number of researchers. \nReal Alto is helping to clarify the nature of the apparent time gap between the very early Las Vegas culture and the later Valdivia culture. In 2016, new radiocarbon dates were published that place Las Vegas culture at 10,800–6600 BP (8,800–4600 BC), and the start of Valdivia culture at 5600 BP (3600 BC). So the Real Alto culture is now considered to belong to the period between these two cultures.\nAround 2500 BCE, the town belonged to the Valdivia culture. At that time, the population grew to about 1,250 individuals. The settlement featured two plazas and two ceremonial mounds.\nOne of their food crops was Calathea allouia.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T18:05:48Z", "lastrevid": 1365390885, "length": 5587, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_Alto", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Real_Alto&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_Alto", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q122746372"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -2.36527778, "lon": -80.65361111, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Real_Alto&params=2_21_55_S_80_39_13_W_"}, {"url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225546868_Root_and_Tuber_Phytoliths_and_Starch_Grains_Document_Manioc_Manihot_Esculenta_Arrowroot_Maranta_Arundinacea_and_Lleren_Calathea_sp_at_the_Real_Alto_Site_Ecuador?_sg=3cEgOCB4UrSrPoBhzdDe8DM7eZOGMBPf_JyDJfJ_BpKixLuvsgReTTLEBiqX27_YZOhR_YTLxsnUQDjT8zWxqg"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20160807231150/http://doaks.org/resources/publications/doaks-online-publications/ecuador/ecuador06.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20160324103009/http://doaks.org/resources/publications/doaks-online-publications/ecuador"}, {"url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222785473_Maize_in_ancient_Ecuador_Results_of_residue_analysis_of_stone_tools_from_the_Real_Alto_site"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140513004945/http://www.doaks.org/resources/publications/doaks-online-publications/ecuador/ecuador05.pdf"}, {"url": "http://journals.cambridge.org/action/displayAbstract?fromPage=online&aid=10326308"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140513005001/http://www.doaks.org/resources/publications/doaks-online-publications/ecuador/ecuador07.pdf"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1017%2Frdc.2015.23"}, {"url": "https://iwannatrip.com/en/Real-Alto-museum/1523"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0033-8222"}, {"url": "https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/real/hd_real.htm"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Real_Alto&params=2_21_55_S_80_39_13_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 76127120, "ns": 0, "title": "SS Prinz August Wilhelm", "extract": "SS Prinz August Wilhelm was a Hamburg America Line (HAPAG) cargo liner that was launched in Germany in 1902 and scuttled in Colombia in 1918. Her original route was between Hamburg and Mexico. From 1906 she served routes between New York and the Caribbean.\nTo avoid capture by the Entente Powers in First World War she sheltered in neutral Colombia from August 1914 until April 1918, when her crew scuttled her to prevent the United States Shipping Board (USSB) from seizing her. Her wreck remains in shallow water in Puerto Colombia. Artefacts recovered from it are in a museum in Barranquilla.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T18:06:10Z", "lastrevid": 1327732749, "length": 16911, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Prinz_August_Wilhelm", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=SS_Prinz_August_Wilhelm&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Prinz_August_Wilhelm", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q124608896"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1905/09/22/101419477.html?pageNumber=6"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1906/06/19/120281410.html?pageNumber=4"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1907/03/01/101024867.html?pageNumber=8"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1907/05/04/106751094.html?pageNumber=1"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1907/05/28/106708732.html?pageNumber=4"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1909/02/26/101867766.html?pageNumber=4"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1911/06/03/105029140.html?pageNumber=11"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1914/08/02/105456186.html?pageNumber=3"}, {"url": "https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1915/11/19/105048084.html?pageNumber=22"}, {"url": "https://www.timetableimages.com/maritime/images/hapag.htm"}, {"url": "https://chroniclingamerica.loc.gov/lccn/sn83030431/1918-05-10/ed-1/seq-4/"}, {"url": "https://chroniclingamerica.loc.gov/search/pages/results/?state=New+York&date1=1914&date2=1918&proxtext=Prinz+August+Wilhelm&x=11&y=11&dateFilterType=yearRange&rows=50&searchType=basic"}, {"url": "https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2018/04/28/la-increible-disputa-detras-del-naufragio-de-un-lujoso-buque-aleman-en-el-caribe-colombiano-durante-la-primera-guerra-mundial/"}, {"url": "https://www.bbc.com/mundo/noticias-america-latina-44152253"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/HECROS1904ST/page/n669/mode/1up"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/HECROS1911ST/page/n840/mode/1up"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/HECROS1913SV/page/n345/mode/1up"}, {"url": "https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/212112058.pdf"}, {"url": "https://read.dukeupress.edu/hahr/article-abstract/96/1/175/36493/De-la-gloria-al-olvido-El-hundimiento-del-vapor"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/1989-4104"}]}, {"pageid": 76476409, "ns": 0, "title": "Salt Mines of Maras", "extract": "The Salt Mines of Maras (Spanish: Salineras de Maras) is one of the largest salt extraction centers of prehispanic origin in Cusco. It is in the Urubamba Province in Cusco in southeast Peru, at an elevation of 3,200 m (10,500 ft) above sea level.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T18:06:16Z", "lastrevid": 1353012203, "length": 8140, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_Mines_of_Maras", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Salt_Mines_of_Maras&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_Mines_of_Maras", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q31386827"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.30027778, "lon": -72.15555556, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6412/"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Salt_Mines_of_Maras&params=13_18_01_S_72_09_20_W_scale:30000"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Salt_Mines_of_Maras&params=13_18_01_S_72_09_20_W_scale:30000_type:city"}, {"url": "https://patrimoniomundial.cultura.pe/sites/default/files/li/pdf/5.%20Salineras%20de%20Maras%20-%20Esp_reduce.pdf"}, {"url": "https://www.machutravelperu.com/blog/maras-salt-mines"}, {"url": "https://andina.pe/ingles/noticia-perus-cusco-tourists-will-be-allowed-to-visit-maras-salt-mines-during-evening-hours-996115.aspx"}, {"url": "https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2025-11-12/high-in-perus-andes-villagers-carry-out-centuries-old-work-of-collecting-salt-in-photos"}]}, {"pageid": 79693537, "ns": 0, "title": "Ratanabá", "extract": "Ratanabá is a conspiracy theory about a lost city in the Brazilian Amazon rainforest allegedly built by extraterrestrial beings. The theory originated with Urandir Fernandes de Oliveira, who claims contact with extraterrestrials, and was spread by gossip pages like Choquei and former Brazilian Culture Secretary Mário Frias.\nThe story has been debunked as false but became a viral meme on Brazilian social media. Archeologists have dismissed the claims, with BBC Brasil reporting the legend \"makes no sense\" scientifically. The Intercept described it as \"pure Bolsonarist lysergia\" emerging shortly after the murders of Dom Phillips and Bruno Pereira.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T18:07:05Z", "lastrevid": 1362962385, "length": 13204, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratanab%C3%A1", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ratanab%C3%A1&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratanab%C3%A1", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q112683795"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://www.gov.br/iphan/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/iphan-repudia-videos-com-informacoes-falsas-sobre-o-forte-principe-da-beira"}, {"url": "https://revistaquestaodeciencia.com.br/apocalipse-now/2022/06/18/ratanaba-capital-do-brasil"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20220624001132/https://www.boatos.org/opiniao/nao-e-coincidencia-teoria-do-ratanaba-viralizar-na-mesma-semana-da-morte-de-dom-phillips-e-bruno.html"}, {"url": "https://www.boatos.org/opiniao/nao-e-coincidencia-teoria-do-ratanaba-viralizar-na-mesma-semana-da-morte-de-dom-phillips-e-bruno.html"}, {"url": "